  <p class="lede">Delta maintains a live, evidence-backed record of what is currently true about
  the things a person follows, and tells them when that record materially changes. The unit it
  delivers is a <b>Change Event</b>: the believed value moved from A to B, it matters, and here
  is the evidence behind it.</p>

  <h2>THE PROBLEM</h2>
  <p>Information about a technical ecosystem is abundant. The expensive work is not finding it.
  It is continuously deciding whether something a person cares about actually changed, whether
  that change matters, and what is different from before.</p>
  <p>Release notes, documentation, advisories, repositories and feeds all produce overlapping
  coverage of the same underlying occurrence. A keyword alert fires on a <em>mention</em>, which
  is not the same as the thing itself having moved. An answer engine will tell you what is true
  now if you ask it now, but it keeps no record of what it told you last time, so it cannot tell
  you what changed in between. Either way the reader has to remember the previous state
  themselves in order to judge whether an update means anything.</p>
  <p>Holding that state is also what makes the awkward cases survivable. A source can retract a
  story it published yesterday. An announcement can arrive days after the event it describes. A
  feed can quietly start returning nothing at all while still reporting success. Alerting that
  keeps no previous state cannot tell any of those apart from a genuinely quiet week.</p>
  <p>The wedge is therefore <b>stateful change detection</b>: identify a material change in
  something specific, show the transition from the previous state, and carry the evidence.</p>

  <h2>WHAT DELTA TRACKS</h2>
  <p>A topic is a subscription to a <b>canonical entity</b>, and the state belongs to the entity
  rather than to each subscriber. This is the structural decision that makes the economics work:
  the expensive processing happens once per entity, however many people follow it.</p>
  <p>An entity is defined by its <b>coordinates</b> &mdash; its identity in someone else's
  namespace, such as a GitHub repository, an OSV package or an endoflife.date product. Fetch URLs
  are composed from those coordinates against allowlisted host templates, so adding an entity is
  adding a row rather than authoring an integration. The same construction closes server-side
  request forgery as a class rather than filtering for it: no URL Delta fetches is ever supplied
  by a user.</p>
  <p>Against each entity Delta holds a small set of <b>typed facts</b> &mdash; the current stable
  version, open advisories, an end-of-life date and a handful of others &mdash; and every entity
  declares which of them it supports. A fact type an entity does not declare cannot enter its
  state and cannot produce a Change Event. That bound is what makes <em>material change</em> something a rule can decide, rather than an open-ended comparison of two documents.</p>
  <p><b>Absence is not removal.</b> If a source stops mentioning an advisory, it has not said the
  advisory ended, so a fact leaves state only on positive evidence. An empty response from an
  advisory API means the query failed, not that there are no advisories &mdash; and treating the
  two as the same thing would turn one bad response into a burst of confident, wrong
  &ldquo;resolved&rdquo; events across every entity that source feeds.</p>

  <h2>THE PIPELINE</h2>
  <p>One observation travels a fixed path from a public source to a delivered Change Event, and
  the work is grouped by the service that performs it.</p>
  <div class="flow">
    <div class="flow-s">
      <h3>delta-ingest</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Picks a source that is due and fetches it with a conditional request, so a source
          that has not changed costs almost nothing to check.</li>
        <li>Validates that the response is healthy, applies the source's storage policy,
          normalizes it into an observation, and fingerprints it so a repeat is recognised.</li>
        <li><span class="tx">In a single transaction</span>, stores that observation together
          with the intent to announce it.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-s">
      <h3>delta-processing</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Parses structured sources deterministically, with no model involved. The model is
          used only for free text, and only where deterministic parsing found nothing.</li>
        <li>Resolves which entity the observation is about by exact match on an external
          identifier &mdash; never by similarity.</li>
        <li>Groups observations that describe the same real-world occurrence, then decides
          whether the change is material by looking it up in a rule table.</li>
        <li>Compares the result against what Delta currently believes about that entity.</li>
        <li><span class="tx">In a single transaction</span>, updates the fact, writes the Change
          Event and its evidence, and writes the intent to announce it.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-s">
      <h3>delta-delivery</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Scores the event against each subscriber's preferences, applies each channel's
          delivery policy, and sends it in-app and by email.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>Steps one to eight run <b>once per entity and read no user data at all</b>. Per-user work
  begins only at step nine, after a Change Event already exists. That invariant is what keeps the
  cost of a popular topic flat as its audience grows.</p>
  <p>Step seven has three outcomes rather than two, and the third is easy to miss. If the value
  differs, a new Change Event is created. If the value is unchanged but the observation belongs
  to a fact Delta already holds, it is recorded as <em>corroboration</em> &mdash; the evidence and
  confidence are strengthened and no event is raised. If it neither differs nor belongs to
  anything known, nothing is recorded. All three mark the observation as processed, because an
  observation that produced no claim may still have been handled perfectly. Plenty of legitimate
  observations contain nothing material.</p>

  <h2>WHERE THE MODEL IS ALLOWED TO ACT</h2>
  <p>The model is an <b>uncertain parser, never an authority</b>. Structured sources are parsed
  deterministically. AI extraction runs on free text only, and only when deterministic parsing
  has produced nothing.</p>
  <p>Four constraints are enforced in code rather than in prompt wording:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Output must satisfy a strict schema; a parse failure is retried once, then abandoned.</li>
    <li>The fact type must already be one the entity supports, so the model cannot widen the
      state schema by inventing a new kind of fact.</li>
    <li>A verbatim evidence quote must be present in the stored source content. A claim whose
      quote cannot be found in the source is dropped as ungrounded.</li>
    <li>Claims below the extraction-confidence threshold are kept as candidate evidence and are
      never promoted into state.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Model-reported confidence is treated as a rough ordering rather than a real probability, and
  it may only ever cause a claim to be <em>rejected</em>. High confidence never buys a claim past
  the schema, the fact-type check or the evidence quote.</p>
  <p>Explanation generation runs after the Change Event has already been decided, and cannot
  alter materiality, state or the diff. If it fails, the event is created with a deterministic
  template summary instead. Prose is never on the correctness path. A user's preferences never
  enter a prompt at all &mdash; relevance is arithmetic over a small profile, so personalisation
  costs no inference and no user data ever reaches a model.</p>

  <h2>THE OUTBOX</h2>
  <p>Writing the new state to the database and announcing it on Kafka are two separate
  operations, and either can fail without the other. One order of failure is worse than the rest.
  The database commit succeeds, so the new value is now what Delta believes. The publish to Kafka
  then fails, so nothing downstream ever hears about it.</p>
  <p>Retrying does not rescue it. Re-running the same observation compares it against the state,
  which already holds the new value, finds no difference, and correctly concludes there is
  nothing to report. The Change Event is now permanently invisible: it happened, the record of it
  is right, and no subscriber will ever be told. Sweeping for stragglers afterwards does not find
  it either, because an entity with no pending work looks identical to an entity whose
  observations genuinely produced nothing.</p>
  <p>A <b>transactional outbox</b> removes the failure rather than compensating for it. The
  intent to publish is written inside the same transaction as the state change, so the two either
  both exist or neither does. If the process dies at any point afterwards, the outbox row
  survives and a publisher retries it. Publication becomes at-least-once with no possibility of
  loss, and consumers are idempotent, so a repeat is harmless.</p>
  <p>It is also what makes a modest single-broker Kafka acceptable here: PostgreSQL holds both
  the authoritative record and the intent to publish, so Kafka's contents can always be
  re-derived.</p>

  <h2>GUARANTEES STATED AS THEY ARE</h2>
  <p>In-app delivery is effectively once. Delivery state is a database row and the feed is the
  source of truth, so a missed real-time frame is reconciled the next time the feed is read.</p>
  <p>Email is <b>at least once</b>, because a database row can be idempotent while an external
  side effect cannot: the provider can accept a message and the process can die before the
  delivery row is updated. Delta narrows that window by recording the attempt before making it,
  and closes it entirely where the provider supports idempotency keys, using a key derived from
  the user, the event and the channel. Where it cannot be closed, a duplicate is preferred to a
  miss &mdash; a missed material change destroys trust, and a duplicate email is an annoyance.</p>
  <p>Delivery failure never blocks state maintenance, because delivery sits downstream of truth.</p>

  <h2>FAILING CLOSED</h2>
  <p>Delta is a trust product, so uncertainty must never harden into an assertion. If the model
  is unavailable, semantic work waits and no state is invented. A low-confidence extraction is
  kept as candidate evidence rather than promoted. An observation about an entity that cannot be
  resolved is stored but produces no claim. Sources that contradict each other keep the
  contradiction and defer the transition rather than picking a winner. An event that is later
  disproven is marked disputed, then retracted with the evidence that overturned it, and the
  history shows that it happened.</p>
  <div class="pull"><p>If something breaks while Delta is working out what is true, it stops and
  records nothing. If something breaks while telling you about it, it keeps going and
  retries.</p></div>
  <p>The most dangerous failure carries no error at all. A source can return HTTP 200 with a body
  that is stale, empty or malformed, and from the outside that is indistinguishable from a quiet
  week. So a fetch is validated rather than trusted: transport failures, bodies that do not
  parse, and a source that has gone quiet for longer than its expected cadence all mark it <b>degraded</b>.</p>
  <p>The distinction that matters is between a healthy source with nothing to report and a source
  that has stopped reporting. The first is entirely normal &mdash; most sources are quiet most of
  the time, and that quiet is real information. The second only resembles it. A degraded source
  is surfaced to operators and to users, and is excluded from any conclusion that nothing
  changed, so the fact that it said nothing is never counted as evidence that nothing
  happened.</p>
  <p>One alarm covers what no other signal can. If sources are healthy and yet no Change Event
  has been produced anywhere for twenty-four hours, something has quietly stopped working. Every
  other measurement confirms that the machinery ran; only this one asks whether the machinery
  found anything.</p>

  <h2>ARCHITECTURE</h2>
  <p>Five application services and one static bundle. <code>delta-ingest</code> schedules and
  fetches sources; <code>delta-processing</code> turns observations into state changes; <code>delta-delivery</code> applies per-user relevance and sends; <code>delta-api</code> serves
  every read, write and WebSocket frame; <code>delta-cdc-consumer</code> writes the reporting
  projection; and <code>delta-web</code> hands out the compiled client and nothing else.</p>
  <p>Two decisions shape the deployment. <b>Kubernetes runs compute, not state</b> &mdash;
  application services, the model runtime and observability run in k3s, where independent
  scaling, rollout isolation and failure isolation are genuinely useful, while PostgreSQL, Kafka,
  Redis and Debezium run alongside on the same host under a container runtime. Running stateful
  systems in Kubernetes adds operator complexity, storage orchestration and failure modes that a
  single-node deployment gains nothing from. And <b>in-app delivery is not a direct call</b>:
  delivery publishes to a Redis channel per user, and whichever API replica happens to hold that
  socket forwards it, which is why Redis sits between them rather than beside the API.</p>

