    <div class="dg-frame" id="dgFrame" role="button" tabindex="0"
         aria-label="Enlarge the architecture diagram">
      <span class="dg-zoom" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
             stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <path d="M10 2h4v4M6 14H2v-4M14 2l-5 5M2 14l5-5"/>
        </svg>ENLARGE
      </span>
      <ArchDiagram />
    </div>
    <figcaption>Observation to delivery. Solid amber is the main path; dashed lines are the
    static bundle and the CDC branch. The upper region is the k3s cluster; the lower one is the
    host container runtime outside it.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>KAFKA AND CDC BOUNDARIES</h2>
  <p>Kafka carries two things and no more: observations from ingestion to processing, and Change
  Events from processing to delivery. User feedback and delivery receipts stay in PostgreSQL,
  where nothing consumes them. Keeping the broker this narrow is deliberate &mdash; every
  additional topic is another ordering and replay problem to reason about.</p>
  <p>An event and the retraction that later withdraws it travel the same topic, and both are
  keyed by the entity they concern. Kafka preserves order within a partition and routes one key
  to one partition, so the retraction is always queued behind the event it retracts and cannot
  overtake it. A subscriber can never be told that an event was withdrawn before being told it
  existed.</p>
  <p>Consumers are idempotent, retries are bounded, and a message that keeps failing is moved to
  a dead-letter topic along with the error that stopped it. Replaying from there is a deliberate
  operator action and never automatic, because automatic replay simply recreates the loop that
  caused the failure in the first place.</p>
  <p>Change data capture is kept as a separate, bounded path: Debezium watches only the state and
  Change Event tables and feeds an analytics-shaped consumer. The rule around it is absolute
  &mdash; <b>CDC propagates database rows, domain events drive behaviour, and the two never
  trigger the same effect.</b> The moment both could cause a notification, every effect in the
  system has two possible causes.</p>
  <p>That capability has a real operational cost, and it is accepted knowingly. PostgreSQL will
  not recycle a write-ahead log segment newer than the oldest replication slot's bookmark, so a
  slot with nothing consuming it pins the log, accumulates without bound, and eventually stops
  the database accepting writes. Slot lag is monitored and bounded, and a slot left idle is
  dropped rather than parked.</p>

  <h2>OPERATING IT</h2>
  <p>Infrastructure is defined in Terraform, separated into layers by how often each one changes
  and how much damage destroying it would cause. OpenTelemetry instruments every service, with
  Prometheus for metrics, Loki for logs and Tempo for traces, so a single trace follows one
  observation from fetch through extraction, resolution, materiality and the state comparison all
  the way to delivery, crossing the message broker intact. The two questions that dominate
  debugging are why something reached a user and why an observation produced nothing, and both
  are answered by following one trace end to end.</p>
  <p>Two of the measurements are product measurements rather than operational ones. The number of
  Change Events suppressed, and the number of state promotions blocked, measure the mechanisms
  the whole design exists to provide. If suppression sits near zero, the filtering is not
  filtering anything and the premise has not been demonstrated.</p>
  <p>On the security side, identity is OIDC-compatible and no passwords are stored. Network
  policy is default-deny, and only ingestion, the model gateway and delivery are allowed outbound
  internet access &mdash; the processing service has none, so content arriving from a source has
  no route back out. The runtime database account cannot alter schema; migrations run under a
  separate account, so a compromise at the application layer cannot reshape the database. Source
  content is treated as data and never as instruction, and the defence against prompt injection
  is the structural validation described earlier rather than any wording in a prompt.</p>

  <h2>PROVING IT</h2>
  <p>The first thing built is the benchmark, not the infrastructure. The hardest claim Delta
  makes is that it can produce a correct before and after out of messy evidence, and everything
  else is engineering arranged around that claim.</p>
  <p>Two questions are measured separately, because they fail for different reasons and a single
  score would hide one behind the other.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Is the diff right?</b> Frozen historical cases are replayed with their recorded source
      responses, and the output is checked against both the Change Events that should appear and
      the ones that should not.</li>
    <li><b>Can the fact taxonomy represent the change at all?</b> This is asked against a list of
      genuinely material changes compiled before the extractor existed, by someone who was not
      building it &mdash; otherwise the taxonomy ends up graded by its own author.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A few bars are absolute, because for those the right answer is not a matter of degree: the
  before-state must be correct on every event produced, materiality must match the rule table
  exactly, one real-world occurrence must yield exactly one event, and a window in which nothing
  happened must produce nothing. Recall is deliberately not fixed in advance. A target chosen
  before anyone knows what the taxonomy actually covers is a number invented to be met, so it is
  calibrated from the first run instead.</p>
  <p>The fixtures have to include the awkward cases or the exercise proves nothing: a retraction,
  a release that slipped and was re-dated, an advisory published before the event it describes,
  two entities that share an alias, one occurrence reported by five different sources, and a
  stretch of time where the correct output is silence.</p>
  <p>Alongside all of it, a plain keyword-alerting baseline runs against exactly the same
  fixtures and its results are published next to Delta's. That comparison is the point of the
  whole exercise. If Delta cannot clearly beat a keyword alert on noise while matching it on
  recall, then the premise the product rests on has not been demonstrated &mdash; and it is
  better to learn that from a benchmark than from users.</p>
</main>

